{
	"id": "why-compose",
	"text": "Now that we can create and run a few primitive Effects, it's time to compose them into larger and more meaningful programs. In the preceding lessons, I've hacked my way around this by running one Effect and then separately running another. If Effect has anything to offer us, it's not down this path. We want to combine our effects without forfeiting their blueprintness. Ideally, our entire program will be composed into a single Effect that is run once at the very top of our application.",
	"code": "import { Effect } from \"effect\"\n\nconst sensorData = Effect.promise(() => fetch(\"/api/sensors\").then(r => r.json()))\nconst shout = (msg: string) => Effect.sync(() => console.log(msg))\n",
